  "method": "Plan-view SHAPE computed from the Main Injector TDR (src:fermilab-geo-mi-tdr-1994), then PLACED on the ground with the TDR's own published site coordinates (release pass, gaps stream); the ring is not traced in OSM. (1) Lattice from the TDR: eight straight sections in the order MI-60, -62, -10, -22, -30, -32, -40, -52 with lengths 138, 52, 69, 52, 138, 52, 69, 52 m (ch. 2 s2.1: 'Straight sections MI-10 and MI-40 are 69 m long (two cells), straight sections MI-22, -32, -52, and -62 are 52 m long (one and one half cells), and straight sections MI-30 and MI-60 are 138 m long (four cells)'), each flanked on both sides by 4 dispersion-suppressor half-cells of 12.9665 m (two 4.1 m dipoles each); the arcs are made of normal half-cells of 17.2886 m (two 6.1 m dipoles each). The number of normal half-cells between successive straights is 2, 12, 38, 2 for each half ring (counted from the dipole positions in the MAD lattice listing in the TDR's appendix; the second half repeats it by the TDR's 'twofold rotational symmetry'). Summed length 3319.414 m against the TDR's 'Circumference 3319.419 m' (Table 1-1). (2) Bending: the dipoles (216 x 6.1 m + 128 x 4.1 m, one 'Dipole Field 17.2 kG') are treated as one field, so each bends by its length / rho with rho = 1842.4 m / 2 pi = 293.2 m; heading was integrated along the sequence (bend spread evenly over each half-cell). The path closes on itself. (3) Self-check against a TDR statement that was NOT used to build the shape: 'The line from MI-52 to MI-62 is offset 28.789277 m from the MI-60 straight section and parallel to it'; the reconstruction gives offsets of 28.74 m and 28.89 m (mean 28.82 m, 0.1% from the TDR) and a direction within 0.02 degrees of MI-60. (4) Placement. The TDR appendix \"Main Injector Ring Site Coordinates (MI_19)\" is a MAD survey listing of every lattice element in Fermilab site coordinates (X = site north, Z = site east, metres; checked against the NuMI TDR beam sheet, whose first row Q606 is the same point as lattice element M_606, 97163.814 ft north = 29615.590 m). The lattice origin (the middle of the MI-60 straight section) is at X = 29606.625709 m, Z = 30942.395446 m with heading theta = 2.596497 rad. The scanned listing is OCR-damaged; the 89 rows whose digits read cleanly (element, arc length, X, Z) were used. A rotation plus translation (no scaling) of the shape above to those 89 site-coordinate positions leaves an rms residual of 0.34 m (maximum 0.52 m), and puts the lattice origin 0.5 m from the listed one. The site coordinates are converted to WGS 84 with the Fermilab Site Coordinate System of Beams-doc-1148 (origin A0 = 41°50′14.312704″ N, 88°15′41.143123″ W, false x = y = 100000 ft, +y at geodetic azimuth 38°16′48.01429″, scale 1.000029251; a rotated tangent plane on the GRS80 ellipsoid, within 0.2 m of the double stereographic projection near A0). Result: the MI-60 straight of the drawn ring has geodetic azimuth 339.6 deg; Beams-doc-1148 Appendix A gives its FSCS azimuth as 301°13′53.9″, which is 339.51 deg geodetic (0.1 deg, 1.7 m over 1 km, inside the fit residual). Independent confirmations that were not used in the fit: (a) the 8 GeV line survey in the same appendix (P8_GEV) ends at DUSAF x = 99699.211 ft, y = 97317.148 ft, which falls 1.2 m from this ring at path length 588 m, inside the MI-10 straight (570.5 to 639.7 m); the same point is 9.6 m from the earlier placement, at a position in the arc before the straight; (b) the start of that line (the Booster extraction point, x = 99511.383 ft, y = 99309.844 ft) falls 0.9 m from the 75.44 m radius circle of the Booster ring (circumference 474 m) drawn on this map, which validates the conversion to WGS 84; (c) the same conversion places the upstream end of the MINOS enclosure (NuMI TDR beam sheet) 79 m from the OSM MINOS Service Building, and the MINOS paper says the detector cart is pulled about 80 m from the shaft base into the hall. Replaces an earlier placement by a fit to the OSM service road 'Indian Creek Road', which a ring road cannot fix in rotation: it was displaced 38 to 74 m (mean 54 m) from this one and rotated by 5.7 degrees. Sampled every 12 m (278 vertices, chord error 0.06 m); haversine on R = 6371008.8 m measures 3317.4 m, 0.06 % below the TDR circumference (the validator's sphere is smaller than the ellipsoid here). The tunnel is drawn from the design lattice; as-built deviations of a few metres are possible, so accuracy_m is 8.",
  "name": "Fermilab Main Injector ring (shape from the TDR lattice, placed with the TDR site coordinates)",
